infographic list-column-simple-vertical-arrow
data
  title Why blobs in the database fail — structurally
  desc One convenience (row + bytes in one transaction) bought with four structural failures of the relational engine
  lists
    - label Backups and restores balloon
      desc Every byte of every file rides in the DB backup — a 5 GB schema becomes a 5 TB backup; restores take hours and point-in-time recovery becomes impractical
    - label The buffer cache is poisoned
      desc Large image bytes evict the hot index and row pages that keep queries fast, so structured queries slow down because RAM is full of bytes read once
    - label Replication and WAL bloat
      desc Every insert/update ships the blob down the replication stream — a 1 MB image becomes 1 MB of write-ahead log per write, spiking lag and bandwidth
    - label Connection pool starvation
      desc Streaming a blob holds a precious DB connection for the whole transfer — a handful of concurrent downloads exhausts the pool and starves every other query
theme
  palette #f43f5e #f59e0b #8b5cf6 #6366f1
